<h2>The Website Redesign Decision Framework: Refresh, Rebuild, or Start Fresh</h2>

<p>Not every outdated site needs a complete teardown. Sometimes a targeted refresh is enough. Here is how to decide.</p>

<h3>When to Refresh (Targeted Updates)</h3>

<p>A refresh makes sense when your site is 1-3 years old, built on a modern platform, and underperforming in just 1-2 areas. Typical refresh projects include:</p>

<ul>
  <li>Updating the homepage hero section and CTAs</li>
  <li>Compressing images and fixing Core Web Vitals</li>
  <li>Adding a sticky mobile call bar</li>
  <li>Expanding thin service pages with content</li>
  <li>Adding schema markup</li>
  <li>Redesigning the contact form</li>
</ul>

<p>Cost: $2,000-$8,000. Timeline: 2-4 weeks.</p>

<h3>When to Partially Rebuild (Template + Custom)</h3>

<p>A partial rebuild is right when your site is 3-5 years old, has some solid content, but needs structural and design overhauls. You keep what works and rebuild what does not.</p>

<ul>
  <li>New design system (colors, fonts, components) applied to key templates</li>
  <li>Restructured navigation and site architecture</li>
  <li>Mobile-first responsive rebuild of all templates</li>
  <li>New conversion elements (forms, CTAs, social proof)</li>
  <li>Performance optimization (hosting, images, code)</li>
  <li>SEO migration with 301 redirects</li>
</ul>

<p>Cost: $8,000-$20,000. Timeline: 6-10 weeks.</p>

<h3>When to Start Fresh (Complete Rebuild)</h3>

<p>A complete rebuild is necessary when your site is 5+ years old, built on outdated technology, or failing across all four categories (design, performance, conversion, SEO).</p>

<ul>
  <li>Outdated CMS or proprietary platform that is hard to update</li>
  <li>Non-responsive design that would require rebuilding every template</li>
  <li>Fundamental structural problems (flat architecture, duplicate content, broken URLs)</li>
  <li>Security issues (no SSL, outdated PHP, vulnerable plugins)</li>
  <li>Brand has evolved significantly since the site was built</li>
  <li>Site was built by an amateur and has technical debt throughout</li>
</ul>

<p>Cost: $15,000-$40,000. Timeline: 10-16 weeks.</p>

<h3>The 5-Point Scoring System</h3>

<p>Use this simple framework to score your site:</p>

<ol>
  <li><strong>Design:</strong> Does it look modern and professional? (Score 0-2)</li>
  <li><strong>Performance:</strong> Does it load fast and pass Core Web Vitals? (Score 0-2)</li>
  <li><strong>Conversion:</strong> Does it convert at or above industry benchmarks? (Score 0-2)</li>
  <li><strong>SEO:</strong> Is organic traffic growing and are you in the Local Pack? (Score 0-2)</li>
  <li><strong>Technology:</strong> Is the CMS modern, secure, and easy to update? (Score 0-2)</li>
</ol>

<ul>
  <li><strong>8-10 points:</strong> Refresh only. Your foundation is solid.</li>
  <li><strong>5-7 points:</strong> Partial rebuild. Fix the weak areas.</li>
  <li><strong>0-4 points:</strong> Full rebuild. The foundation is compromised.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Redesign ROI: What the Data Says</h3>

<p>A 2025 industry analysis of 284 companies across sectors found that construction and AEC businesses see an average 340% ROI on website redesigns, with an 8.3-month payback period. The $20,000-$40,000 budget range emerged as the sweet spot for most B2B service companies.</p>

<p>For contractors specifically, the math is compelling. A $15,000 redesign that increases your conversion rate from 2% to 6% on 2,000 monthly visitors generates 80 additional leads per month instead of 40. At a 30% close rate and $8,000 average ticket, that is 12 additional jobs worth $96,000 in revenue per month. The redesign pays for itself in the first month.</p>

<p>Even conservative estimates tell the same story. A roofing contractor investing $12,000 in a redesign that improves conversion from 3% to 5% on 1,500 monthly visitors gains 30 extra leads per month. At a 25% close rate and $12,000 average job, that is 7.5 additional jobs worth $90,000 monthly. The redesign ROI is 650% in month one.</p>

